WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.674
Network-facing behavior is a major part of
IoT device design.

2
00:00:03.754 --> 00:00:06.684
A device may expose local services,
connect to a gateway,

3
00:00:06.764 --> 00:00:10.488
communicate with a cloud service, accept
mobile application messages,

4
00:00:10.568 --> 00:00:12.765
or exchange telemetry and configuration.

5
00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:17.057
Every network path should have a purpose,
an owner, and a deployment expectation.

6
00:00:17.137 --> 00:00:19.518
Local services should match the product
need.

7
00:00:19.598 --> 00:00:22.528
A service used for onboarding, discovery,
maintenance,

8
00:00:22.608 --> 00:00:26.454
or normal operation should be documented
with its allowed build profiles,

9
00:00:26.534 --> 00:00:30.440
access expectations, data types, resource
limits, and shutdown behavior.

10
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:34.610
Services that are not needed in a field
build should not remain enabled by habit.

11
00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:36.522
Protocol framing must be explicit.

12
00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:40.081
The device should define message
boundaries, maximum frame sizes,

13
00:00:40.161 --> 00:00:43.030
allowed states, version behavior, and
error handling.

14
00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:47.444
A parser should validate lengths before
storing data and should reject incomplete,

15
00:00:47.524 --> 00:00:51.675
malformed, or out-of-state messages
without corrupting later protocol state.

16
00:00:51.755 --> 00:00:55.234
Authentication and authorization decisions
belong before trust.

17
00:00:55.314 --> 00:00:57.939
A device should know whether a peer,
service, user,

18
00:00:58.019 --> 00:01:00.522
or local tool is allowed to request an
operation.

19
00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:04.936
Authorization should be specific to the
action and current device state rather than

20
00:01:05.016 --> 00:01:07.763
treating all successful connections as
equivalent.

21
00:01:07.843 --> 00:01:12.238
Secure transport at a high level protects
sensitive communication from unnecessary

22
00:01:12.318 --> 00:01:16.042
exposure and helps the device authenticate
the service it is talking to.

23
00:01:16.122 --> 00:01:20.028
Developers should still handle certificate
lifecycle, time assumptions,

24
00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:22.794
reconnect behavior, and error handling
carefully.

25
00:01:22.874 --> 00:01:25.621
A library call alone does not define the
whole policy.

26
00:01:25.701 --> 00:01:29.425
Timeouts, retry limits, and resource
limits protect device stability.

27
00:01:29.505 --> 00:01:32.862
Network conditions can be slow,
intermittent, or inconsistent.

28
00:01:32.942 --> 00:01:36.727
The device should bound queued work,
parser state, connection attempts,

29
00:01:36.807 --> 00:01:41.141
retained messages, and diagnostic output
so communication failures do not consume

30
00:01:41.221 --> 00:01:42.564
resources indefinitely.

31
00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:45.269
Network input remains untrusted until
validated.

32
00:01:45.349 --> 00:01:48.584
Messages may be malformed, incomplete,
duplicated, delayed,

33
00:01:48.664 --> 00:01:53.120
unexpected for the current state, or
incompatible with the running firmware version.

34
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.595
Defensive protocol handling keeps the
device predictable by focusing on validation

35
00:01:57.675 --> 00:01:59.140
and safe state transitions.
